 Kowalski visits the Coral Reef Research Center in Florida to study corals and their habitats. She is also a member of the Coral Reef Watch Network (CRI) and has been involved with the CRI since 2013.
The Coral Reef Watch Network (CCRN) is an international network of scientists who work on coral reef ecosystems around the world. The CCRN was established by the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) in 1987 as a response to the threat of climate change caused by human activities such as deforestation, pollution, and land clearing for agriculture.
In 2015, the CCRN began its first year of operation, which included five years of research and field experience. In 2016, the CCRN expanded its membership to include more than 400 scientists from over 100 countries.
The CCRN’s mission is to:
- Investigate the effects of climate change on coral reef ecosystems;
- Identify threats to coral reef ecosystems;
- Develop strategies to protect coral reefs;
- Promote sustainable management of coral reef resources;
- Provide scientific information about coral reef ecology and ecosystem services;
- Improve understanding of coral reef ecosystems;
- Increase knowledge
